Note = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {The nature is a space, which we, people, did not create. This is the
   reason why it will never be a really cognizible and absolutely
   understandable or examined place. In spite of the fact that we prefer
   living in an artificial, to humankind adapted environment, we went on to
   make up an environment even more artificial the virtual cyberspace.
   Overusing of technologies of today clearly defines our affection towards
   machines and tools as extension of our own existence. Mankind is no
   longer absolutely dependant on the nature. Creation of the culture
   manifests our new home. In spite of that it seems that our affection for
   the nature stays universal. As the internet developed, it generated new
   kinds of experiences, encounters and innovations. All this tools and
   designs needed names. Many of the names we gave them drew upon metaphors
   from the natural world, as part of the haphazard. It seems there could
   be a connection between our passion for cyberspace and our affection for
   the natural world. Our subconscious has already imprinted nature into
   cyberspace. The aim of the article, using the methods of analysis,
   description and synthesis, is to explain why is this happening and to
   ask, if our biophilia is transformed into a new form of technobiophilia.},
