   NETWORK, DEC 15-17, 2021},
Organization = {Int Soc Hort Sci, Div Landscape \& Urban Hort; Int Soc Hort Sci, Div
   Protected Cultivat \& Soilless Culture; Int Soc Hort Sci, Working Grp
   Urban Hort; Univ Catania, Dept Agr, Food \& Environm; Natl Res Council,
   Res Inst Terrestrial Ecosystems; Int Soc Hort Sci, Working Grp Landscape
   Hort},
Abstract = {The issue of protection of natural environment, including green spaces
   within the cities, is becoming increasingly important both in general
   terms and in relation to the current concerns of Italian cities. These
   issues are also relevant by a perspective of urban planning history and
   in relation to the important line of studies dedicated to the history of
   the agricultural landscape. Agricultural landscape and historical urban
   landscape can be effectively analysed in integrated perspective, which
   considers the evolution of Italian cities from the Middle Ages to the
   late modern history, and the long coexistence of green spaces and built
   spaces inside the historic cities. These topics are currently linked to
   the sustainable development of urban suburbs, to the new cultivation
   systems in urban environment, and to the indispensable interventions -
   even in urban contexts - of biodiversity safeguard. The authors aim to
   highlight - also through an iconographic excursus - how the city of
   Bologna is, in this context, an important case study, which can be
   analysed in interdisciplinary perspective.},
