   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc;
   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {The relationships between satellite observed and ground based
   measurement data within an urban area are analytically estimated.
   Satellite remote sensing data enable to improve this understanding by
   assessing the thermal urban environment as well as for defining heat
   islands in urban areas. In this study, the thermal environment of Seoul,
   megacity in Korea, is examined using satellite images provided by the
   Landsat satellite corresponding to the daytime and normal atmospheric
   condition when the urban heat island (UHI) phenomenon is best observed.
   Pixel-base satellite derived parameters with GIS are integrated to
   estimate spatial analysis for assessment of the UEQ. Based on integrated
   data with land cover, vegetation fraction, and air quality, the spatial
   structure of the urban environment quality (UEQ) is analyzed in each
   case study and the best and worst areas within the urban settings are
   identified. The integration of more environmental information will
   provide a more detailed characterization of the state of urban
   environment than a single index approach.},
