Pages = {269-278},
Note = {13th International Conference on Modelling, Monitoring and Management of
   Air Pollution, Cordoba, SPAIN, 2005},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol},
Abstract = {The vehicle-originated air pollution has a significant impact on the
   ambient air quality in urban areas and creates a certain amount of
   health risk to the public. Especially in urban street canyon
   environments, high levels of air pollution are frequently observed due
   to the increased traffic volume and reduced natural ventilation by
   building forests. The dispersion of vehicle emissions in urban
   environments is also limited by various factors, which may produce poor
   air qualities in urban street canyon environments. This poor air quality
   has a high potential to be easily delivered into the indoor air
   environment by building ventilation.
   In this study, the dispersion of vehicle emissions was characterized by
   employing wind tunnel tests and tracer gas techniques. The aspect ratio
   of a street canyon (i.e. the ratio of the width of a street and the
   average height of buildings) and the direction of external wind are the
   major test parameters.},
